 of Agent-Environment Co-Evolution\n\nChenzhi Zheng 1*Jingwen Lin 1*Jiang-Ming Yang 2\n\nXin Zhang 2 Jianshe Li 2 Yongqi Tong 2\n\n1 University of Birmingham 2 Ant International\n\ncxz477@student.bham.ac.uk\n\njxl2044@student.bham.ac.uk tongyongqi.yq@ant-intl.com\n\nAbstract\n\nWhen agents leave behind tools, memo-\n\nries, code, data, or policies, task suc-\n\ncess alone no longer characterizes evalua-\n\ntion. Reset protocols remain reproducible,\n\nbut they discard inherited state and can-\n\nnot measure whether an agent improves, de-\n\ngrades, or burdens the environment used by\n\nlater agents. Using a mechanism-driven 133-\n\nreference corpus, we survey agent-environment\n\nco-evolution across benchmarks, environment\n\ndesign, tool and memory creation, software\n\nagents, synthetic-data feedback, multi-agent\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Systematic Survey of Self-Evolving Agents: From Model-Centric to Environment-Driven Co-Evolution | TechRxiv (https://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.177203250.05832634/v2)\nciteturn98search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; However, traditional agent systems rely heavily on human-guided training. ... To unify this rapidly expanding field, we propose a taxonomy that categorizes methods into three core components of an autonomous evolutionary cycle: (i) Model-Centric Self-Evolution, where agents enhance internal capabilities through inference scaling or parameter bootstrapping; (ii) Environment-Centric Self-Evolution, where agents achieve continual self-evolution by interacting with the environment to obtain external knowledge and experience-based feedback; and (iii) Model-Environment Co-Evolution, where agents and their environments jointly evolve through sustained interaction.We highlight Model-Environment Co-Evolution as a key emerging direction for Self-Evolving Agents, where environment definitions and their co-evolution with agents will become central challenges in future research. ... Scite shows how a scientific paper has been cited by providing the context of the citation, a classification describing whether it supports, mentions, or contrasts the cited claim, and a label indicating in which section the citation was made.\n\ne-Prints posted on TechRxiv are preliminary reports that are not peer reviewed. They should not be regarded as conclusive, guide clinical practice/health-related behavior, or be reported in the media as established information.\n\n27 February 2026\n\nV2Latest version\n\n# A Systematic Survey of Self-Evolving Agents: From Model-Centric to Environment-Driven Co-Evolution\n\nAuthors: Zhishang Xiang 0009-0009-2034-4904 xiangzhishang@stu.xmu.edu.cn, Chengyi Yang 0009-0006-4953-3459, Zerui Chen, Zhimin Wei, Yunbo Tang, Zongpei Teng, Zexi Peng, … Show All … , Zongxia Li, Chengsong Huang, Yicheng He, Chang Yang, Xinrun Wang, Xiao Huang, Qinggang Zhang, and Jinsong Su Show FewerAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.36227/techrxiv.177203250.05832634/v2\n\n5,397 views\n\n4,573 downloads\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - zongqing0068/awesome-co-evolution: A curated paper list for co-evolution in agentic systems · GitHub (https://github.com/zongqing0068/awesome-co-evolution)\nciteturn98search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; A curated paper list for co-evolution in agentic systems ... Agent–Agent Co-Evolution studies joint evolution among agents in an otherwise fixed environment. ... Agent–Environment Co-Evolution extends the evolutionary process to environments containing task, feedback, and interaction spaces. ...   * 2026 · MAAC — Learning Decentralized LLM Collaboration with Multi-Agent Actor Critic ...   * 2025 · MARS — MARS: Co-evolving Dual-System Deep Research via Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning ...   * 2026 · SIA — SIA: Self Improving AI with Harness & Weight Updates\n-------------------------------------